For the **three Node.js services we created (`user-service`, `product-service`, `order-service`)**,
 I would structure the Helm chart so that **one Helm release deploys all three services**.


$ ls
helm/  helm-config/  helm-temp/  k8s/  order-service/  package-lock.json  product-service/  user-service/

$ helm create ecommerce1.0
Creating ecommerce


## 1. Helm chart structure

```text
nodejs-microservices/
│
├── user-service/
│   ├── src/
│   ├── package.json
│   └── Dockerfile
│
├── product-service/
│   ├── src/
│   ├── package.json
│   └── Dockerfile
│
├── order-service/
│   ├── src/
│   ├── package.json
│   └── Dockerfile
│
└── helm/
    └── ecommerce1.0/
        ├── Chart.yaml
        ├── values.yaml
        │
        └── templates/
            ├── user-deployment.yaml
            ├── user-service.yaml
            ├── product-deployment.yaml
            ├── product-service.yaml
            ├── order-deployment.yaml
            ├── order-service.yaml
            └── ingress.yaml
```

The key idea is:

```text
                  Helm
                   │
                   ▼
             ecommerce chart
                   │
       ┌───────────┼───────────┐
       ▼           ▼           ▼
     User        Product      Order
   Deployment   Deployment   Deployment
       │           │           │
     Service     Service      Service
```

