# 17. Upgrade the application

Suppose you build version `1.1` of `order-service`.

Build:

```bash
docker build \
  -t REGION-docker.pkg.dev/PROJECT_ID/microservices/order-service:1.1 \
  ./order-service
```

Push:

```bash
docker push \
  REGION-docker.pkg.dev/PROJECT_ID/microservices/order-service:1.1
```

Change:

```yaml
services:
  order:
    tag: "1.1"
```

Then:

```bash
helm upgrade ecommerce ./helm/ecommerce
```

Kubernetes performs a rolling update.

```text
Order Pod v1
     │
     ▼
Order Pod v1.1
     │
     ▼
Order Pod v1.1
```

You don't have to manually delete the old Pods.

---

# 18. Scaling

Change:

```yaml
services:
  order:
    replicas: 5
```

Then:

```bash
helm upgrade ecommerce ./helm/ecommerce
```

Kubernetes creates:

```text
order-service
     │
     ├── Pod 1
     ├── Pod 2
     ├── Pod 3
     ├── Pod 4
     └── Pod 5
```

And the Kubernetes Service distributes traffic across them.

---

# 19. Uninstall everything

Because this is one Helm release:

```bash
helm uninstall ecommerce
```

This removes the resources created by this release.

That's one of the major advantages of Helm.

